foreign  [Music]  for Thursday November 17th I'm Zoe  Thomas for The Wall Street Journal  in September Russian President Vladimir  Putin ordered a mass mobilization of men  to support the country's war in Ukraine  that order spurred many men of fighting  age including from its Tech sector to  leave the country  it's actually the second recent Exodus  of tech workers from Russia a lot of  them left last winter when the invasion  was announced but this is believed to be  a far bigger wave of Departures as those  who couldn't leave last time now look  for an exit so what does this mean for  Russia's struggling economy and what  happens next for these highly skilled  workers  joining us to discuss all of this is our  reporter Yulia chernova hi Yulia thanks  for joining me thank you Yulia some  estimates show as many as a million  people left Russia this spring that  includes software developers and other  Tech workers that can do their jobs  remotely so where are they going the  biggest beneficiary is probably Georgia  and then other countries that offer an  easier transition whether they have a  visa-free entry or an easy way of  obtaining a Visa so Kazakhstan is a big  beneficiary than you have in Europe  Serbia is another destination that's  pretty large there is interesting  research by professor at Vienna  University of economics and business who  looked at GitHub accounts of Russian  developers GitHub is a software platform  where developers collaborate  and a hegeol located the accounts of  Russian developers to see whether they  are listing a new location for  themselves so what he found was that as  of November 8th about 13 of Russian  developers Now list a different country  than they did at the start of this year  and another 13 just obscured their  location and he estimates that about 20  percent of Russian developers have left  the country this year and the biggest  destination according to his research is  Georgia  and how easy is it for these Tech  workers to to find a new job or are they  keeping the positions that they had when  they were still in Russia a lot of them  actually are keeping their jobs some of  the Russian companies did adjust their  own policies to allow their workers to  continue remotely at the same time a lot  of the people I spoke with are trying to  find other jobs and it's not that easy  to get paid abroad you know they're  getting paid in rubles in Russia and  transferring that money out isn't simple  especially with all kinds of sanctions  and problems with the monetary  transactions cross-border right now with  Russia and in general there's a lot of  uncertainty in the Russian economy there  are reasons not to be tied closely to  these Russian companies so these workers  are looking as best they can what does  that mean for the you know startups or  Russian tech companies that have hired  these workers you know how easy is it  for the companies to survive once their  employees are leaving the country there  was a big dislocation and panic and  pause and just work itself by many Tech  teams in Russia right around the time  when mobilization was announced some say  that by now things have settled down and  people are adjusting and having workers  abroad is somewhat manageable for these  companies but it also depends on what  kind of work and what kind of projects  they are performing if they're working  for the government let's say it's harder  to justify having data transferred  across borders let's say or are any of  these companies having to consider  shutting down or moving their operations  entirely out of Russia yes we certainly  saw a lot of companies move their own  operations abroad I spoke with one  company for example in drive it's a ride  sharing startup that was founded in  Siberia in Russia currently it's based  in Silicon Valley it's backed by  American investors and most of their  workers were based in Russia they had  more than a thousand at the beginning of  this year and they are relocating  everybody out of the country this  company in particular has a lot of  people moving to Kazakhstan and Cyprus  whoever is not leaving the CEO said will  be laid off can you give us a sense of  how important the tech sector is to  Russia's economy and I guess how that  has changed since the start of the war  and since this new Exodus of tech  workers the tech sector is certainly  important for the Russian economy it's  estimated between three and six percent  of the country's GDP it was one of the  faster growing sectors of the economy  and it was also a sector that helped the  economy  diversify the Russian economy is very  much dependent on oil and gas revenue  and the tech sector was also important  in supplying a lot of well-paid jobs so  certainly the departure from the country  is a hit to the consumer market and in  general it's also a tax hit the income  taxes that these folks were paying to  the treasury are likely not going to be  paid in Russia and also Tech is  important to the government it supports  other sectors and the development of  other sectors and it's also important  just for the defense industry for cyber  security so then what does this kind of  brain drain mean long term for Russia  and its Ambitions within Tech you have  hundreds of thousands of smart educated  motivated well-off people leaving the  country it's a loss for Russia and again  for a wherever they end up at the same  time it's not to say that everything  stopped and all the tech companies  closed in Russia they are still growth  there and some companies are doing okay  and some of these companies are doing  even better than they thought they would  be back in the spring so it remains to  be seen what happens but the general  uncertainty is a big problem for the  tech sector because Founders are scared  to invest further all right that's our  reporter Yulia chernova thanks so much  for joining us thank you  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing if you want more Tech stories  check out our website  wsj.com I'm Zoe Thomas for The Wall  Street Journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]

